1
00:00:00,155 --> 00:00:00,475
<u>Kotlin</u>

2
00:00:01,817 --> 00:00:03,517
<i>What is the difference between val and var?</i>

3
00:00:04,786 --> 00:00:08,126
A val reference cannot be reassigned after initialization, while a var can.

4
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:13,626
However, val does not make the referenced object immutable: the contents of a mutable collection can still be changed.

5
00:00:15,134 --> 00:00:17,124
<i>What is the difference between lateinit and lazy?</i>

6
00:00:18,646 --> 00:00:23,686
lateinit var is initialized manually later, and reading it too early throws UninitializedPropertyAccessException.

7
00:00:24,396 --> 00:00:28,356
val by lazy is initialized automatically on first access, and the result is cached.

8
00:00:29,886 --> 00:00:30,976
<i>What are nullable types?</i>

9
00:00:32,515 --> 00:00:36,455
In Kotlin, nullability is part of the type system: String cannot be null, while String?

10
00:00:37,165 --> 00:00:37,605
can.

11
00:00:38,295 --> 00:00:42,575
This reduces the risk of NullPointerException and requires missing values to be handled explicitly.

12
00:00:44,446 --> 00:00:46,306
<i>What is the difference between == and ===?</i>

13
00:00:48,711 --> 00:00:50,631
== checks structural equality and calls equals().

14
00:00:52,251 --> 00:00:55,791
=== checks referential equality, meaning whether both references point to the same object.

15
00:00:57,866 --> 00:00:59,516
<i>What is the difference between as and as??</i>

16
00:01:01,035 --> 00:01:04,785
as performs a regular cast and throws ClassCastException when the type is incompatible.

17
00:01:05,485 --> 00:01:05,925
as?

18
00:01:06,655 --> 00:01:08,605
returns null instead of throwing an exception.

19
00:01:10,134 --> 00:01:11,184
<i>What is a data class?</i>

20
00:01:12,715 --> 00:01:14,465
A data class is intended to hold data.

21
00:01:15,165 --> 00:01:21,505
Kotlin automatically generates equals(), hashCode(), toString(), copy(), and componentN() functions for properties from the primary constructor.

22
00:01:23,369 --> 00:01:25,919
<i>What is the difference between a sealed class and an enum class?</i>

23
00:01:27,538 --> 00:01:30,438
An enum class represents a fixed set of constants of the same type.

24
00:01:31,138 --> 00:01:34,058
A sealed class or sealed interface represents a restricted hierarchy

25
00:01:34,078 --> 00:01:35,658
where each variant can hold its own data.

26
00:01:36,906 --> 00:01:39,396
<i>What is the difference between object and companion object?</i>

27
00:01:40,894 --> 00:01:42,244
object declares a singleton.

28
00:01:42,954 --> 00:01:48,244
A companion object is an object associated with a class and is used for static-like members, factory methods, and constants.

29
00:01:49,777 --> 00:01:51,257
<i>What does emptyList() return?</i>

30
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:55,070
emptyList() returns an empty read-only List<T>.

31
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:59,690
Elements cannot be added to it; use mutableListOf() when a mutable list is required.

32
00:02:01,198 --> 00:02:03,218
<i>What is the difference between List and MutableList?</i>

33
00:02:04,734 --> 00:02:08,034
List exposes read-only operations, while MutableList allows changes.

34
00:02:08,734 --> 00:02:13,914
However, List does not guarantee true immutability of the underlying object; it only restricts the available API.

35
00:02:15,441 --> 00:02:16,701
<i>What is an extension function?</i>

36
00:02:18,243 --> 00:02:23,403
An extension function adds convenient method-like syntax to an existing type without inheritance or changing the original class.

37
00:02:24,093 --> 00:02:27,773
Under the hood, it is a regular function and cannot access private members of the class.

38
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:30,600
<i>What is a higher-order function?</i>

39
00:02:32,107 --> 00:02:35,577
A higher-order function takes another function as a parameter or returns a function.

40
00:02:36,287 --> 00:02:39,237
Kotlin uses them heavily in collections, Flow, and DSLs.

41
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:42,295
<i>What is inline used for?</i>

42
00:02:43,570 --> 00:02:47,270
inline allows the compiler to insert the function body and lambda code directly at the call site,

43
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:49,170
reducing the overhead of lambda objects.

44
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:53,170
It is especially useful for small higher-order functions and reified generics.

45
00:02:54,681 --> 00:02:55,981
<i>What is reified used for?</i>

46
00:02:57,507 --> 00:03:00,767
reified keeps generic type information available inside an inline function.

47
00:03:01,477 --> 00:03:05,787
This makes operations such as T::class and is T possible without passing a Class or KClass explicitly.

48
00:03:07,326 --> 00:03:09,196
<i>Is Kotlin Int a primitive or an object?</i>

49
00:03:10,723 --> 00:03:13,093
At the language level, it is a regular Kotlin type.

50
00:03:13,793 --> 00:03:19,263
On the JVM, it is usually compiled to primitive int, while nullable and generic contexts require boxing into Integer.

51
00:03:20,777 --> 00:03:22,557
<i>Which Kotlin type has no instances?</i>

52
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:26,713
Nothing has no possible values and cannot be instantiated.

53
00:03:27,413 --> 00:03:32,263
It is used as the return type of functions that never complete normally, for example functions that always throw an exception.

54
00:03:33,782 --> 00:03:35,992
<i>What does a sealed class become in JVM bytecode?</i>

55
00:03:37,515 --> 00:03:40,625
It becomes a base JVM class or interface plus separate subclasses.

56
00:03:41,335 --> 00:03:44,575
The Kotlin compiler enforces the hierarchy restrictions and exhaustive checks;

57
00:03:44,745 --> 00:03:47,309
modern JVM targets may also include sealed-type metadata.
